- op: discuss  # 3f9f
  at: '2026-05-12T09:27:28Z'  # 3f9f
  by: agent  # 3f9f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3f9f
  clock: 3  # 3f9f
  nonce: 3f9f  # 3f9f
  message: "Closed as not-a-bug after investigation 2026-05-12.\n\nPremise of the WI ('this is a fresh tier-filtering bug specific to TS/JS supply-chain classification') was incorrect. byte-identical slice.tier1 / slice.tier2 is the structurally correct result when the slice's BFS reach doesn't extend into tier-2 code.\n\nInvestigation:\n- Existing TEST_FILE_PATTERNS already covers .spec.[jt]sx? and .test.[jt]sx? colocated test files: nestjs's packages/<pkg>/src/**/*.spec.ts files ARE classified tier-1 with is_test=True (matches the WI-pugas Phoenix pattern but for TS/JS).\n- nestjs (OSS framework repo) tier-2 candidates per EXAMPLE_PATTERNS: sample/, samples/. The MqttController slice rooted at packages/microservices/.../mqtt-controller.ts has no inbound edges to sample/* (production code doesn't import sample apps).\n- integration/* directories in nestjs contain e2e tests but the dir name is not in any test/example pattern; they classify as tier-1. Adding generic 'integration/' to TEST_DIR_PATTERNS is risky (legitimate repos use integration/ for production integration code).\n- Production code paths don't typically import test code, so any slice rooted at production code will have an empty tier-2 BFS reach.\n\nConclusion: byte-identical tier1/tier2 means the slice's BFS doesn't reach tier-2 code from this entry. The right tier filter is doing nothing-additional for this particular slice; this is correct, not a bug.\n\nAdjacent opportunity (NOT pursued here, deferred): the bakeoff-deep harness could dedup the slice.tier{1,2}.json artifacts when identical to save bytes and signal 'tier-2 unreached' to consumers. Filed as future polish — no code change in this closure.\n\nWI-pugas (the analogous Phoenix item) DID find a real bug in Elixir test classification and is closed separately."  # 3f9f
